course: language
lesson: 25
title: Idioms taken literally
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An idiom is a group of words whose meaning is not the meaning of its separate words; you have to learn it as one whole piece, like a key that opens one door.
explain: Grandad looks out and says "It's raining cats and dogs!", and little Kai runs to the window to look for falling pets; Grandad only meant it is raining very hard.
explain: Every language has idioms, and they sound mad when translated word for word: a Ukrainian gran says "вішати локшину на вуха", "to hang noodles on someone's ears", which means to tell them nonsense.
explain: People love telling stories about where idioms came from, but many of those stories are guesses; when nobody is sure, a good speaker says "maybe".
fact: An idiom is a phrase with a special meaning that is different from the meaning of its words.
fact: "Raining cats and dogs" means raining very hard; nobody knows for sure where it came from, and the stories about animals falling from roofs are not proven.
fact: In theatre "Break a leg!" means "Good luck!"; actors thought saying "good luck" was unlucky, and the exact origin is not known.
fact: "Beat about the bush" means avoid saying something directly; it comes from hunters beating bushes to scare birds out.
fact: "Cost an arm and a leg" means cost a lot of money; it is a deliberate exaggeration, called hyperbole.
fact: "Bite the bullet" means face something unpleasant bravely; the story about soldiers biting bullets is often told but not proven.
fact: "Hit the nail on the head" means be exactly right; it comes from carpentry, where a nail hit exactly goes straight in.
fact: In British football talk, "sick as a parrot" means very disappointed and "over the moon" means very happy.
fact: The Ukrainian idiom "вішати локшину на вуха" (hang noodles on ears) means to tell someone lies or nonsense; to translate an idiom, find the matching idiom, not the matching words.
fact: "Pull someone's leg" means tease or trick them for fun.
fact: "Let the cat out of the bag" means reveal a secret; the story about a cat swapped for a pig at a market has no clear proof.
fact: In Scots, "Away and bile yer heid!" (go and boil your head) means "go away" or "don't talk nonsense"; it is cheeky, not a real threat.
fact: "I'm all ears" means "I'm listening carefully"; other body-part idioms are "keep an eye on", "give someone a hand" and "a heart of gold".
exercise: Grandad says "It's raining cats and dogs!" What does he mean? | It is raining very hard | Pets are falling from the sky | It is raining a little bit | The cats and dogs are wet
exercise: Mum says "Break a leg!" before Ruby's school play. What does she mean? | Good luck | Be careful on stage | Don't come home late | Try to fall over
exercise: Coach says "Let's not beat about the bush, you lost." What is he doing? | Saying it straight, without avoiding the point | Asking the team to tidy up the garden behind the clubhouse | Telling them to hunt birds | Asking them to run round a bush
exercise: Dad says the phone "cost an arm and a leg". What does he mean? | It was very expensive | It was cheap | It hurt him to buy it | It was a bargain
exercise: What is hyperbole? | Deliberate exaggeration | A kind of question | A short poem | A whispered secret
exercise: At the dentist Mum says "Bite the bullet, love." What does she mean? | Be brave about something unpleasant | Bite on a hard sweet | Keep your mouth closed | Don't cry or you'll be told off by the dentist
exercise: Leo's teacher says "You've hit the nail on the head!" What has Leo done? | Got exactly the right answer | Hurt his thumb with a hammer in woodwork | Built a shelf | Made a silly mistake
exercise: A footballer says "We were sick as a parrot." How does he feel? | Very disappointed | Very happy | Ill in bed | Very hungry
exercise: Ukrainian Gran says "вішати локшину на вуха". What does she mean? | Telling someone lies or nonsense | Making noodle soup for the whole family | Wearing big earrings | Listening carefully
exercise: Which English idiom means teasing or tricking someone for fun? | Pull someone's leg | Break a leg | Cost an arm and a leg | Give someone a hand
exercise: "Don't let the cat out of the bag" before a surprise party means what? | Don't tell the secret | Don't lose the cat | Don't open the presents | Don't let anyone leave
exercise: A girl from Glasgow says "Away and bile yer heid!" to her brother. What does she mean? | Go away, don't talk nonsense | I'm really going to boil your head | Come and have some soup | Go and wash your hair in hot water
exercise: Mum says "I'm all ears." What does she mean? | I'm listening carefully | I have lots of ears | I can't hear you | My ears hurt
exercise: How should you translate an idiom into another language? | Find the matching idiom, not word for word | Translate each word exactly, one by one, in order | Leave it out completely | Say it louder
exercise: "Over the moon" in football talk means what? | Very happy | Very disappointed | Very tired | Very far away
exercise: What is an idiom? | A phrase whose meaning isn't the meaning of its words | A word spelled the same both ways, like "level" | A long word made of two shorter words, like "football" | A word borrowed from French
test: Your mate goes "animals really fell off roofs in storms, that's where cats and dogs comes from, fr." ("Fr" means for real.) What's fairest? | That story is not proven; nobody is sure of the origin | He's right, it's proven history, written down in old newspapers | It came from a cartoon in 2010 | It comes from a real storm in Paris
test: Ruby's little bro cries "Mum wants Ruby to get HURT!" What would you tell him? | In theatre "break a leg" means good luck | He's right, Mum is cross about the school play | It's about a broken chair | It means "dance faster"
test: What would happen if you translated "hang noodles on ears" word for word into English for a friend? | They'd picture real noodles and miss the meaning | They'd understand it at once | It would turn into "pull your leg" all by itself | It would mean exactly the same in every language
test: Your cousin from Newcastle says "howay man, that trainer cost an arm and a leg, canny dear." ("Howay" means come on; "canny dear" here means quite expensive.) What did the trainer cost? | A lot of money | Very little money | His arm | Nothing, it was free
test: A Gen Z teen says "lowkey you hit the nail on the head, bestie." ("Lowkey" means a bit, quietly.) What does she mean? | You got it exactly right | You hurt your hand | You need a hammer | You told a lie
test: In a cartoon a man literally bites a bullet at the dentist. What's the real meaning of the idiom? | Face something unpleasant bravely | Eat something metal | Keep your teeth clean and shiny every day | Shout at the dentist
test: Theo's mate goes "just spit it out, stop beatin' round the bush, bruv." What does he want? | Say it directly | Go and play in the garden | Spit on the ground | Run round the bushes
test: Zoe asks "do parrots get sick after football?" What would you say? | "Sick as a parrot" just means very disappointed | Yes, parrots hate football | It means the team has a pet parrot that watches | It means the players were ill
test: An English cousin is scared when a Glasgow girl says "away and bile yer heid". What should he understand? | It's a cheeky "go away", not a threat | She really means to hurt him | It's a recipe | It means "come here quickly"
test: Your dad says "Let the cat out of the bag" came from real market tricks, 100 percent. What's fair? | It's a popular story, but there's no clear proof | Yes, it's fully proven by old market records from London | It came from a film | It came from Latin
test: Sam laughs "you've only got two ears, Mum!" Which idiom works the same way, by picturing body parts? | Give someone a hand | Over the moon | Raining cats and dogs | Sick as a parrot
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an idiom? | A fixed expression whose meaning is not derivable from the literal meanings of its constituent words | A word whose spelling is identical when reversed | A word borrowed from another language without alteration | A grammatical error tolerated in informal speech
exam: Identify the meaning conveyed by the expression "to cost an arm and a leg". | To be exceedingly expensive | To result in bodily injury | To be obtainable without charge | To require great physical exertion
exam: In respect of the origin of the expression "raining cats and dogs", select the response consistent with the established facts. | Its origin is uncertain and the popular explanations are unproven | It is proven to derive from animals falling from the roofs of houses in storms | It was coined by Shakespeare in 1600 | It derives from a Latin weather proverb
exam: Determine the most appropriate procedure for rendering an idiom into another language. | The substitution of an idiom of equivalent meaning in the target language | The literal translation of each constituent word in its original order, without exception | The omission of all figurative language | The retention of the original words untranslated
